"executive_summary": "This feature article reviews the development of bioinspired supramolecular materials designed to mimic dynamic bio-interfaces critical for cellular activities. The authors highlight three primary areas of innovation: the construction of artificial extracellular matrices (ECMs) using self-assembling peptide hydrogels to enhance neuronal migration in injured brains; the creation of peptide fibres capable of efficient incorporation and sustained release of growth factors like VEGF for tissue regeneration; and the engineering of stimuli-responsive systems, including redox-controlled cargo release and highly stable phospholipid nanosheets for extended blood circulation. Additionally, the article describes a molecular machine approach to induce endocytosis-like fission in liposomes for the efficient encapsulation and in vivo delivery of viruses. By combining supramolecular chemistry with nanotechnology, these materials aim to reconstruct the dynamic nature of natural bio-interfaces, offering new strategies for regenerative medicine and advanced drug delivery systems. The review underscores the potential of bottom-up methodologies to manipulate biological interfaces, moving from static scaffolds to dynamic, functional systems that actively participate in cellular processes such as adhesion, signal transduction, and substance transportation.",
